---
import Marketing from '@layouts/Marketing.astro';

// The bottom scroll-spy status line's four sections. Ids match the heading
// ids inside `body` below; labels are what the tmux-style bar prints.
const scrollspy = [
  { id: 's-short', label: 'short' },
  { id: 's-table', label: 'table' },
  { id: 's-cost', label: 'cost' },
  { id: 's-not', label: 'not-for-you' },
];

// Body markup re-skinned onto the new .pagehead/.prose/.tbl-wrap+table.ledger/
// .callout system (see moshpit.css). The prose itself — every capability
// claim, every price — is kept word-for-word from the docshell version this
// replaces, checked against the vendors' own pages on 2026-08-07. This pass
// only changes how it's drawn, not what it says.
const body = `<header class="pagehead">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="kicker">COMPARE</div>
    <h1>Where Moshpit fits, and where it doesn't.</h1>
    <p class="sub">iPhone terminals are a crowded shelf, and several of them are very good
    at things Moshpit does not attempt. This page is the version of that comparison we
    would want to read before paying for something: capability by capability, with the
    cases where you should buy a competitor instead stated plainly rather than buried.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<main class="wrap">
  <div class="prose" style="max-width:none">
    <h3 id="s-short" style="margin-top:0">The short version</h3>
    <p>Moshpit is built for one job: <b>your coding agents can reach you</b>. An agent
    blocks on a permission prompt, your lock screen says so, and you answer it there.
    Everything else — SSH, Mosh, tmux, herdr — exists so that job works on a real
    terminal instead of a toy one. If that is not your job, a different app is probably
    a better buy, and the last section says which.</p>

    <h3 id="s-table">Capability by capability</h3>
    <p>Columns are categories rather than named products, because features and prices
    move and a table that names names goes stale and unfair within a release. The
    pricing row is the exception — those figures were checked directly, and dated.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table class="ledger">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Capability</th><th class="us">Moshpit</th>
          <th>Subscription terminals</th><th>One-time tmux clients</th><th>Agent-first apps</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="cap-col"><b>Answer an agent from the lock screen</b><br>Approve, deny or reply without unlocking into the app</td>
          <td class="us yes">Allow · Deny · Reply</td><td class="no">—</td>
          <td class="no">Connection alerts only</td><td class="no">Varies — several do this well</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="cap-col"><b>Agent status with no host-side setup</b></td>
          <td class="us yes">Built in on herdr</td><td class="no">—</td><td class="no">—</td>
          <td class="no">Usually needs a hook or agent integration</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="cap-col"><b>Voice input, transcribed on the device</b><br>Dictate a prompt, read it, then insert it — the audio never leaves the phone</td>
          <td class="us yes">On-device only</td><td class="no">—</td><td class="no">—</td>
          <td class="no">Dictation via the system keyboard</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="cap-col"><b>Mosh</b><br>Survives Wi-Fi/5G handoff, sleep, packet loss</td>
          <td class="us yes">Native</td><td class="no">Sometimes</td>
          <td class="no">Usually absent</td><td class="no">Sometimes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="cap-col"><b>tmux rendered natively</b><br>Not a shrunken desktop</td>
          <td class="us yes">Control mode</td><td class="no">Varies</td>
          <td class="no">Yes — this is their core</td><td class="no">Varies</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="cap-col"><b>herdr support</b></td>
          <td class="us yes">Yes</td><td class="no">—</td><td class="no">—</td><td class="no">Rare</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="cap-col"><b>Start an isolated task from the phone</b><br>New git worktree, new workspace, agent started in it</td>
          <td class="us yes">One sheet</td><td class="no">—</td><td class="no">—</td><td class="no">Rare</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="cap-col"><b>Data collected</b></td>
          <td class="us yes">None — no account, no telemetry</td>
          <td class="no">Account required</td><td class="no">Varies</td><td class="no">Varies</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="cap-col"><b>Pricing model</b></td>
          <td class="us yes">One-time purchase</td><td class="no">Recurring, indefinitely</td>
          <td class="no">One-time</td><td class="no">Mostly subscription</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="max-width:none">
    <h3 id="s-cost">What the field actually costs</h3>
    <p>Checked on the App Store and the vendors' own pages on <b>2026-08-07</b>. Prices
    change and regional pricing differs — treat this as a snapshot, not a live quote, and
    check the store before you buy anything.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table class="ledger">
      <thead><tr><th>App</th><th>Model</th><th>Price when checked</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="cap-col">Termius</td><td>Freemium + subscription</td><td class="no">Pro about $10/month; free tier has no cloud sync</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="cap-col">Blink Shell</td><td>Subscription (source is GPL)</td><td class="no">Blink+ about $19.99/year; Build add-on about $7.99/month</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="cap-col">Prompt 3 (Panic)</td><td>Subscription</td><td class="no">About $9.99/year, with a trial</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="cap-col">Secure ShellFish</td><td>Free + one-time unlock</td><td class="no">About $29.99 lifetime</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="cap-col">Moshi</td><td>Subscription + lifetime</td><td class="no">About $7.99/month, $69.99/year, or $199 lifetime</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="cap-col">Attach</td><td>One-time, no in-app purchases</td><td class="no">About $4.99</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="cap-col us"><b>Moshpit</b></td><td class="us">One-time, no in-app purchases</td><td class="us yes">$6.99 launch → $9.99 standard</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="max-width:none">
    <p>The launch price is a real launch price, not a struck-through discount: Moshpit
    has never sold at a higher number, so there is nothing honest to cross out. It rises
    to $9.99 once the first wave of buyers is in. Buying at $6.99 includes every update
    after it, at no extra cost.</p>

    <h3 id="s-not">When you should <em>not</em> choose Moshpit</h3>
    <p>These are real, and they are the reason to read this page rather than the front
    one. If any of them is your situation, one of the apps above is the better purchase
    and we would rather you bought it than returned ours.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <h4>Better served elsewhere</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><b>You want a mature product with years of reviews.</b> Moshpit is new. There is
      no track record to check, no long changelog, and no community answering questions at
      2am. Termius, Blink, Prompt and Secure ShellFish have all been shipping for years.</li>
      <li><b>You need file management as a first-class feature.</b> Moshpit is a terminal.
      If your day is SFTP, editing remote files in a GUI, or mounting a server into the
      Files app, Secure ShellFish is built precisely for that and Moshpit is not.</li>
      <li><b>You want the same subscription to cover desktop.</b> Moshpit is iPhone and
      iPad only, bought once, with no Mac or web client. Termius and Prompt cover several
      platforms under one account.</li>
      <li><b>You need Android.</b> There is no Android version and none planned.</li>
      <li><b>You want an app that syncs hosts and keys across devices for you.</b> Moshpit
      has no account and no cloud, which is the point — but it also means moving to a new
      phone means adding your hosts again.</li>
      <li><b>You do not run agents.</b> If you just want a solid SSH client, everything
      Moshpit is unusual at is wasted on you, and the one-time tmux clients are cheaper
      and perfectly good.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="callout ok">
    <h4>Clearly the right one</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>You run Claude Code, Codex or similar on your own machines, and you are tired of
      finding an agent that stopped hours ago waiting for a yes.</li>
      <li>You use herdr, or you would if a phone client supported it.</li>
      <li>You work from trains, cafés and other places where the network moves — Mosh is
      built in, not an add-on.</li>
      <li>You would rather buy something once than rent it, and you would rather an app
      collected nothing about you at all.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p class="prose" style="margin:32px 0 60px"><a href="/docs">Read the docs</a> ·
  <a href="/guide/claude-code">See the full walkthrough</a> ·
  <a href="/">Back to the overview</a></p>
</main>`;
---

<Marketing
  title="How Moshpit compares — Moshpit"
  description="An honest comparison of iPhone terminals: where Moshpit is the right tool for agent work, and the cases where another app serves you better."
  path="compare"
  lang="en"
  scrollspy={scrollspy}
>
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Marketing>
